RAWM(1)

# NAME

rawm - Ra window manager for X

# SYNOPSIS

*rawm* [*-C*] [*-c* _path_] [*-v*]

# DESCRIPTION

*rawm* is a dynamic tiling window manager for X, derived from *dwm*
version 6.0.  It manages windows in tiled, monocle bottom stack,
bottom stack horizontal and floating layouts.  Either layout can be
applied dynamically, optimising the environment for the application in
use and the task performed.

In tiled layouts windows are managed in a master and stacking area.
The master area contains the window which currently needs most
attention, whereas the stacking area contains all other windows.

```
tile  ([]= nmaster)
+---------+-------+     Tiled layout is default, but can be
|         |   2   |     selected manually by *Meta-t*.
|         |       |
|    1    +-------+     The "nmaster" indicates the number of
|         |   3   |     clients in master area, and can be
|         |       |     increased/decreased by *Meta-i* / *Meta-d*.
+---------+-------+
master    stacking
area       area
```

Gapless grid layout arranges the windows in a grid.

```
gaplessgrid   (###)
+-----+-----+-----+     NO MASTER AREA!
|  1  |  3  |  5  |
|     |     +-----+     Layout can be selected by *Meta-g*.
+-----+-----|  6  |
|  2  |  4  +-----+
|     |     |  7  |
+-----+-----+-----+
```

In monocle layout all windows are maximised to the screen size.

```
monocle     ([1/4])
+-----------------+     NO MASTER AREA!
|                 |
|                 |     1/4 shows currently selected vs. total
|   1,2,3,4,...   |     number of available windows.
|                 |
|                 |     Layout can be selected by Meta-m.
+-----------------+
```

Bottom stack (TTT) and bottom stack horizontal (===) are two stack
layouts for *rawm*.

```
bstack (TTT nmaster)          bstackhoriz (=== nmaster)
+-----------------+                 +-----------------+
|                 |     master      |                 |
|        1        |   <- area ->    |        1        |
|                 |                 |                 |
+-----+-----+-----+                 +-----------------+
|  2  |  3  |  4  |    stacking     +--------2--------+
|     |     |     |   <- area ->    +--------3--------+
+-----+-----+-----+                 +--------4--------+
```

Layouts can be selected by *Meta-s* (bstack) and *Meta-h*
(bstackhoriz).

In floating layout windows can be resized and moved freely.  Dialog
windows are always managed floating, regardless of the layout applied.

```
float       (<1/3>)
+-----------------+     NO MASTER AREA!
| +-----+ +-----+ |
| |  1  | |  2  | |     1/3 shows currently selected vs total
| +-----+ +-----+ |     number of available windows.
|    +-------+    |
|    |   3   |    |
|    +-------+    |     Layout can be selected by *Meta-f*.
+-----------------+
```

Windows are grouped by tags.  Each window can be tagged with one or
multiple tags.  Selecting certain tags displays all windows with these
tags.

Each screen contains a small status bar which displays all used tags,
the layout, the title (when built with *-Dwindow_title=true*) of the focused
window, and semantic status segments collected by its private status worker
on the focused screen.

The bar is a semantic rail. Viewed tags use the selected authority color as
a filled cell, urgent tags use the urgent authority color, and the layout is
drawn as a neutral mode cell. The focused window title is centered in the
remaining title area and receives a compact border-colored marker on the
workspace-facing edge. Floating focus therefore differs by authority color,
not by a legacy square glyph.

Normal status segments remain quiet. Warning and critical segments become
semantic cells using the urgent and error schemes.

Configured panel actions are discoverable without adding persistent chrome:
interactive tags, layout/title regions, and status segments use the hand
cursor while hovered. Quiet regions receive a short workspace-facing marker;
the focused-title marker widens while the title region is interactive.

When enabled, rawm also presents a transient OSD for interactive state
changes: views and tag assignments, monitor navigation, layout and
master-area changes, floating/fullscreen state, bar visibility, and tag
renaming. The OSD is a rawm-owned compositor surface and never enters normal
client management. Its text is centered within a stable configured minimum
width. Workspace, routing, layout, master-area, and ordinary window-state
feedback use the selected scheme; elevated/floating feedback uses the
floating scheme. When *ui.osd.geometry* is enabled, interactive floating move
and resize operations additionally show throttled live geometry from rawm's
admitted client state rather than raw pointer deltas.

*rawm* draws a small border around windows to indicate the focus state.

The options are as follows:

*-c* _path_, *--config* _path_
	Load the built-in defaults followed by exactly _path_, bypassing the
	standard system and user configuration files.

*-C*, *--check-config*
	Validate the effective configuration and exit without opening the X
	display.

*--status-debug*
	Run the configured status collector in the foreground and print each changed
	private snapshot to standard output. This diagnostic mode does not open X.

*-v*, *--version*
	Print version and exit.

# USAGE

## Status bar

When *status.enabled* is true, rawm starts a private collector worker from the
validated *status.sources* configuration. The worker owns sampling state while
rawm owns rendering, colors, separators, hit testing, and status-specific mouse
bindings.

The worker is an implementation process, not a separately installed service.
It communicates with rawm over an anonymous packet socket. Unexpected worker
death marks current segments stale and schedules a bounded restart without
terminating the window manager.

Each segment carries a stable identifier, text, and one of *normal*, *warning*,
*critical*, or *stale*. The root *WM_NAME* property is not used for status, and
rawm exports no status property for external producers. See *rawm.conf*(5) for
source configuration.

## Control and state interface

rawm owns the per-screen X selection *\_RAWM\_CONTROL\_SN*, where _N_ is the
X screen number. The selection owner is an unmapped rawm-owned window and is the
liveness boundary for the external control/state protocol. If rawm loses its X
connection, X destroys that owner window and clears the selection.

The owner publishes *\_RAWM\_CONTROL\_VERSION*,
*\_RAWM\_SUPPORTED\_COMMANDS*, and the complete authoritative
*\_RAWM\_STATE* snapshot. Consumers that need change notification select
*PropertyChangeMask* on the owner and reread the complete snapshot after state
changes rather than reconstructing rawm tag/layout/focus semantics from EWMH or
the client tree.

Commands are versioned *ClientMessage* events of type *\_RAWM\_COMMAND* sent
directly to the selection owner. The advertised command set contains only
window-management operations. Program spawning, panel visibility, status,
volume, global-key/XKB policy, and session lifecycle are intentionally outside
this interface.

The exact version-1 record grammar, command atoms, and argument semantics are
documented in the source-tree *docs/control-protocol.md* contract.

## Compositor integration

rawm publishes private per-client properties describing window-manager state
that a compositor cannot reliably infer from generic ICCCM/EWMH properties.
They are descriptive outputs only; clients and compositors must not write them
as commands back to rawm.

*\_RAWM\_FLOATING*
	CARDINAL 0 or 1. One means the client is effectively on the floating
	presentation plane, either because the client itself is floating or because
	the monitor currently uses the floating layout. Fullscreen clients also
	report one; compositors should give standard fullscreen state precedence.

*\_RAWM\_SELECTED*
	CARDINAL 0 or 1. One only for the globally selected client on the selected
	monitor. This is rawm's compositor-facing focus authority.

*\_RAWM\_LAYOUT*
	UTF8_STRING naming the selected monitor layout. Stable values are *tile*,
	*floating*, *monocle*, *bstack*, *bstack_horizontal*, and *grid*.

*\_RAWM\_MONITOR*
	CARDINAL monitor number, starting at zero. The same property is also
	published on rawm-owned compositor surfaces.

rawm-owned non-client surfaces additionally publish:

*\_RAWM\_SURFACE*
	UTF8_STRING describing the surface role. Stable values are *bar*,
	*systray*, and *osd*. Their WM_CLASS instances are *rawm-bar*,
	*rawm-systray*, and *rawm-osd* respectively; all use *rawm* as the class
	and advertise *\_NET\_WM\_WINDOW\_TYPE\_DOCK*. This lets compositors
	distinguish desktop chrome and transient rawm feedback from ordinary clients
	without title or geometry heuristics. The systray surface is unmapped while
	empty, while the selected monitor's bar is hidden, or while a visible
	fullscreen client owns that monitor.

The client properties are removed when rawm releases a surviving client.
Private rawm protocol atoms are intentionally not advertised through
*\_NET\_SUPPORTED*, which is reserved for EWMH atoms.

## Keyboard commands

These are the built-in bindings. An *input.keys* list in *rawm.conf*(5)
replaces them.

*Meta-r*
	Start the configured *launcher* command.

*Meta-Shift-Return*
	Start the configured *terminal* command.

*Meta-,*
	Focus previous screen, if any.

*Meta-.*
	Focus next screen, if any.

*Meta-Shift-,*
	Send focused window to previous screen, if any.

*Meta-Shift-.*
	Send focused window to next screen, if any.

*Meta-b*
	Toggles bar on and off.

*Meta-t*
	Sets tiled layout.

*Meta-f*
	Sets floating layout.

*Meta-m*
	Sets monocle layout.

*Meta-s*
	Sets bstack layout.

*Meta-h*
	Sets horizontal bstack layout.

*Meta-g*
	Sets grid layout.

*Meta-space*
	Toggles between current and previous layout.

*Meta-j*
	Focus next window.

*Meta-k*
	Focus previous window.

*Meta-i*
	Increase clients in master area.

*Meta-d*
	Decrease clients in master area.

*Meta-Shift-l*
	Increase master area size.

*Meta-Shift-h*
	Decrease master area size.

*Meta-Return*
	Zooms/cycles focused window to/from master area (tiled layouts
	only).

*Meta-n*
	Set the current tag name using the configured *tag_rename* command.

*Meta-w*
	Select a managed window using the configured *window_select* command.

*Meta-v*
	Open clipboard history using the configured *clipboard_history* command.
	The stock policy runs *rawclip menu*. rawm owns the global key binding but
	does not own clipboard-history capture, exclusion, or retention policy.

*Control-Alt-Delete*
	Invoke the configured *lock* command. In the stock policy this runs
	*rawlock*(1); rawm does not supervise the locker or participate in
	authentication.

*Meta-c*
	Close focused window.

*Meta-Shift-space*
	Toggle focused window between tiled and floating state.

*Meta-Shift-f*
	Toggle focused window between fullscreen and its previous tiled or
	floating state.

*Meta-Tab*
	Toggles to the previously selected tags.

*Meta-Shift-[1..n]*
	Apply nth tag to focused window.

*Meta-Shift-0*
	Apply all tags to focused window.

*Meta-Control-Shift-[1..n]*
	Add/remove nth tag to/from focused window.

*Meta-[1..n]*
	View all windows with nth tag.

*Meta-0*
	View all windows with any tag.

*Meta-o*
	Switch the current view to the focused window's tag set.

*Meta-Control-[1..n]*
	Add/remove all windows with nth tag to/from the view.

*Meta-Shift-q*
	Quit *rawm*.

*Meta-Control-Shift-q*
	Restart *rawm*.

## Mouse commands

These are the built-in mouse bindings. An *input.buttons* list in
*rawm.conf*(5) replaces them.

*Button1* on tags
	View the clicked tag.

*Button3* on tags
	Add/remove the clicked tag from the current view.

*Button4*, *Button5* on tags
	Cycle backward or forward through configured single-tag views.

*Meta-Button1* on tags
	Assign the clicked tag to the focused window.

*Meta-Button3* on tags
	Add/remove the clicked tag on the focused window.

*Button1* on layout
	Toggle between the current and previous layout.

*Button3* on layout
	Select monocle.

*Button4*, *Button5* on layout
	Cycle backward or forward through the stable layout set.

*Button2* on status
	Start the configured *terminal* command.

*Meta-Button1* on client
	Move the focused window while dragging. Tiled windows become
	floating after movement exceeds the snap threshold.

*Meta-Button2* on client
	Toggle the focused window between floating and tiled state.

*Meta-Button3* on client
	Resize the focused window while dragging. Tiled windows become
	floating after resizing exceeds the snap threshold.

# CONFIGURATION

*rawm* loads runtime policy from *rawm.conf*(5). The default search order is
built-in defaults, _/etc/rawm/rawm.conf_, then the XDG user file. Lists such
as rules and input bindings have explicit replacement semantics; named
commands merge by name. String settings and command arguments may reuse final
scalar values with *${path}* references. Use *rawm -C* before restarting after
an edit.

# SIGNALS

*SIGHUP* (1)
	Restart the *rawm* process.

*SIGTERM* (15)
	Cleanly terminate the *rawm* process.

# SEE ALSO

*rawm.conf*(5), *rawmctl*(1), *rawsession*(1), *rawmenu*(1), *rawlock*(1)

